---
/**
 * `/guides/capital-gains` — from `docs/research/08-capital-gains.md` and
 * `docs/personas/p4-investment-cgt-terminal.md`.
 *
 * THE RATE IS DELIBERATELY NOT STATED AS A HEADLINE FIGURE
 *
 * Research 08 opens with "Read this first: the rate in this document is out
 * of date" — the 2017 individual rate has been superseded by the Inland
 * Revenue (Amendment) Act, No. 11 of 2026, which this repository does not
 * hold (Q42). Per CLAUDE.md rule 3 and this prompt's "do not restate a
 * figure that the research marks unverified as though it were settled",
 * this page describes the framework — what counts as an investment asset,
 * the principal-residence exclusion, the relief exclusion, pre-commencement
 * base cost — which research 08 calls "structurally stable", and states the
 * rate only as something this project cannot currently give.
 */
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import AsAtStamp from '../../components/AsAtStamp.astro';
import { Callout, CitationRef } from '../../components/ui/index.ts';
import { loadTaxYears } from '../../lib/tax/load.ts';
import '../../styles/guides.css';

const taxYear = loadTaxYears()[0]!.data;
---

<Base
  title="Do I owe tax on the gain when I sell a house or shares? — Sri Lanka Tax Return Support"
  description="What counts as a taxable gain, the strict conditions on the exemption for a family home, and why this project cannot currently give you the rate."
>
  <div class="guide measure">
    <p class="guide__back"><a href="/guides/">All guides</a></p>

    <h1 class="guide__question">
      Do I owe tax on the gain when I sell a house or shares?
    </h1>

    <p class="guide__answer">
      Generally yes — gains from selling an "investment asset" are taxable — but this
      project cannot currently tell you the rate. The 2017 rate has been changed by a 2026
      amendment act this project does not yet hold, so stating the old figure would be
      stating something known to be wrong.{' '}
      <CitationRef
        src="ira-2017#Sch.1 para 1(2)(a)"
        sources={taxYear.sources}
        label="Superseded rate from"
      />{' '}
      What has not changed is which gains are in scope and which family home sales are exempt,
      and those are strict enough to catch people who reasonably assume they are exempt.
    </p>

    <AsAtStamp
      yearOfAssessment={taxYear.yearOfAssessment}
      dataReviewedOn={taxYear.lastReviewed}
    />

    <Callout
      tone="caution"
      title="The current rate is not available on this site"
      headingLevel={2}
    >
      <p>
        The individual capital gains rate was changed by the Inland Revenue (Amendment)
        Act, No. 11 of 2026 — an act this project does not hold, and does not quote
        figures from.{' '}
        <CitationRef
          src="ira-2017"
          sources={taxYear.sources}
          label="What is confirmed comes from"
        />{' '}
        Reporting elsewhere describes a new figure, but it is not confirmed against that primary
        source, and this project does not repeat an unconfirmed number as though it were the
        rate. Until the act is obtained, the calculator on this site cannot compute a capital
        gains figure at all — it does not guess at a rate it cannot cite.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <section class="guide__section">
      <h2>What counts as a taxable gain</h2>
      <p>
        The charge applies to gains from realising an "investment asset" — a capital asset
        held as part of an investment. Land, buildings, structural improvements, interests
        in land or buildings, and leases are all included.{' '}
        <CitationRef
          src="ira-2017#s.195"
          sources={taxYear.sources}
          label="Definition from"
        />
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="guide__section">
      <h2>The exemption for your own home is stricter than most people assume</h2>
      <p>
        A principal place of residence is excluded from the charge — but only where{' '}
        <strong>both</strong> of these hold, counted on a daily basis:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>you owned it continuously for the three years before you sold it, and</li>
        <li>you lived in it for at least two of those three years.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <CitationRef
          src="ira-2017#s.195"
          sources={taxYear.sources}
          label="Conditions from"
        />{' '}
        Someone who moved out eighteen months before selling, or who bought the property two
        years ago, does not qualify — and would very reasonably expect to. If you inherited
        a property and never lived in it, it is squarely inside the charge, not outside it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="guide__section">
      <h2>Personal relief does not apply to a gain</h2>
      <p>
        The Fifth Schedule states that personal relief "is not available to be deducted
        against gains from the realisation of investment assets".{' '}
        <CitationRef src="ira-2017#Sch.5 para 2(a)" sources={taxYear.sources} />{' '}
        A gain is taxed on its own footing, separately from your other income — it is not pooled
        with your salary or business income and covered by the same relief.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="guide__section">
      <h2>What the gain is measured against, for something you already owned</h2>
      <p>
        For an asset you owned before the Act commenced, its cost for this purpose is
        taken as its <strong>market value on 30 September 2017</strong> — not what was originally
        paid for it.{' '}
        <CitationRef
          src="ira-2017"
          sources={taxYear.sources}
          label="Transitional rule from"
        />{' '}
        For an inherited property held for decades, this usually means a valuation as at that
        date is what the gain is actually measured against, not the purchase price decades earlier
        — and how the cost of an asset acquired specifically by inheritance or gift is treated
        is not yet confirmed by anything this project holds.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="guide__section">
      <h2>What is not yet confirmed</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>The current rate, and when it took effect</li>
        <li>
          The payment deadline on a sale — capital gains commonly run on a short deadline
          from the sale itself rather than from the end of the year, and if that is right
          here it may be the single most useful fact on this page. Not yet confirmed.
        </li>
        <li>Whether a separate return is required for a gain</li>
        <li>
          How a loss on realisation is treated — offset, carried forward, or neither
        </li>
        <li>
          Whether the treatment of motor vehicle disposals has changed, reportedly with
          retrospective effect from 1 April 2024 — which would reach sales already made
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="guide__section">
      <h2>What this means if this is your situation</h2>
      <p>
        Until the current rate is confirmed, this site's{' '}
        <a href="/calculator/">calculator</a> cannot compute a capital gains figure for you
        — entering a gain will show you where the computation stops and why, rather than a number
        that would rest on a rate known to be wrong. Take the occupancy question and the sale
        date to a qualified tax practitioner in the meantime, particularly if a deadline may
        already be running from the date of sale.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="guide__footer">
      <p class="guide__footer-note">
        This is decision support, not tax advice. Verify with the Inland Revenue
        Department or a qualified tax practitioner before you rely on any figure here to
        file or pay.
      </p>
      <ul class="guide__footer-links">
        <li><a href="/calculator/">Work out your figure</a></li>
        <li><a href="/about/sources/">Sources cited on this page</a></li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
